%N A066814 Smallest prime p such that (p-1) has n divisors, or 0 if no such prime exists.
%C A066814 The only primes p for which p-1 has a prime number of divisors are Fermat primes A019434.
%C A066814 From _Michael S. Branicky_, Aug 08 2026: (Start)
%C A066814 a(49) = 0 since it must have one of two forms, both of which are composite:
%C A066814    i. a(49) = p^48 + 1 = (p^16 + 1)(p^32 - p^16 + 1);
%C A066814   ii. a(49) = p^6*q^6 + 1 = x^6 + 1 = (x^2 + 1)(x^4 - x^2 + 1), where x = p*q.
%C A066814 Generalizing this, a(p^k) = 0 for p prime, p-1 = 0 (mod 3), and k >= 2. (End)
%C A066814 From _David A. Corneth_, Aug 09 2026: (Start):
%C A066814 a(91) = 0. Proof: The factorizations of 91 are 91 and 7*13. Subtracting 1 from all factors gives possible multiplicities of primes (90) and (6, 12) respectively. All of these numbers have a gcd larger than 1 and no power of 2 and so for any number k with 91 divisors k can be factored and there are no solutions. (End)
%H A066814 David A. Corneth, <a href="/A066814/b066814.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..1354</a>
%e A066814 a(17) = 65537 because A000005(65536) = 17.
%t A066814 it=Table[ p=Prime[ n ]; DivisorSigma[ 0, p-1 ], {n, 400000} ]; Flatten[ Position[ it, #, 1, 1 ]&/@Range[ 100 ]/.{}- > 0 ]
%Y A066814 Cf. A000005, A004249, A007516, A066529, A072275.
